# 0070-prepare-shaders-in-background-in-the-app.patch -- the main app prepares the built game's shaders BY ITSELF, in the background, while the player is in iBuilder, with a progress bar on the game's
# Library tile/row and in its sheet; Play stays available at every moment. The seed-list run is capped at 4,000 libraries (the player's first start too).
# Applies after 0069 (the tree with 0010..0069 applied). UNTESTED ON DEVICE AND IN THE SIMULATOR: compiled only (dotnet build -c Release -r ios-arm64 of a copy of the generated device project with these
# files: rc 0, 0 errors, 39 warnings, the same 28 distinct warnings as that copy built without the patch: rc 0, 0 errors, 39). The pure part (WarmCap, PrepareCount, PrepareText, the cap keeping the seed
# list's order through Order) ran in a host check (GameAppWarm.cs compiled with WARM_LOGIC_ONLY: 11 checks). No run on an iPhone: nothing here is measured yet.
#
# WHY (the owner, paraphrased: "let the compilation run in the background while the player is in the iBuilder app, with a progress bar, and the player can launch the game at any moment"; everything
#   automatic on the phone). Field facts, iPhone 15 Pro, Ocarina of Time (Switch 2, 42,848 shaders, pso_manifest.tsv 25,163 lines):
#   * at each Play the host compiled the head of the seed list from Metal source before the first frame: 13,066 lines = 9,124 libraries, 1,061-1,708 s on 6 workers, 200-1,800 ms a library on a cold
#     cache, 1-4 ms on a hit;
#   * iOS's Metal compile cache (per bundle id) kept only about 5,500-5,800 of this game's libraries: in every later session the first ~5,800 were hits and everything after was compiled again. Warming
#     more is wasted: hence the cap;
#   * a separate change (Rust, the host) builds only ~500 lines before the first frame and the rest during play. What this patch buys: those later on-demand compiles become 1-4 ms hits.
#
# WHAT IT DOES
#   * GameAppWarm.cs, GameWarmLogic: WarmCap = 4000 (comment: the ~5,500-5,800 seen kept), PrepareCount(listed) = min(listed, WarmCap), PrepareText(done, total) = "Preparing the shaders… 1,200 of 4,000"
#       (invariant thousands separators).
#   * GameAppWarm.cs, GameWarm.Run: the seed-list mode (firstStart: true; NeededKeys = manifest, then pso_user.tsv, then the used keys) takes the first PrepareCount(front) libraries in that order (it took
#       them all: ~25k keys for Ocarina in a player's first start). The main app's seed-list run shows PrepareText; an exported player keeps "Preparing the game (once)…". A seed-list run that finds
#       nothing to compile writes prepared.tsv too (else every launch would read the pack to find nothing). Start/Run take an optional `why` that opens the first log line, e.g.
#       `shader warm-up: started by itself after the build of <game>: 4000 libraries of <tid>'s seed list (cap 4000; the list, the learned and the used ones name 25210) to compile into Metal's cache,
#       4 workers; ...`. Workers, QoS and waits unchanged for that mode: 4 workers at UserInitiated, wait at thermal Serious+, wait below 700 MiB available.
#   * GameAppWarm.cs, view controller:
#       - PrepareAfterBuild(outcome): after a build whose outcome is StopOutcome.Finished (not boot-only, not failed, paused or cancelled) -> AutoPrepare.
#       - StartWarmAtLaunch(): besides the debug argument, in the main app only: if gameapp.args, gameapp.continue or gameapp.boot-only exists at this point (the launch will build, continue or boot;
#         an ATTEMPTED boot-only is skipped too) it logs `shader warm-up: not resumed at this launch (...)` and does nothing; else after 3 s (first frame, the queue's QueueResumeIfOwed) -> AutoPrepare.
#       - AutoPrepare: main app, no run, no warm-up going; the game whose framework is Fresh (GameStatus.Ready); its title id; on a worker: HasLibrary + FirstStartPending (no prepared.tsv for this
#         app build + chain); back on the main thread: still no run, no warm-up, no build queue owed (buildQueue.AutoStart) -> StartWarmTitle(tid, game, firstStart: true, why). A finished run's
#         prepared.tsv means it never runs again for this app build: this is not the whole-library review at every launch that 0020 removed.
#       - StopWarm (a build or boot starts, BeginRun): also ends the warm-up's ticker and its continued-processing task at once (BackgroundEndWarm), because BeginRun asks for the build's task right
#         after and BackgroundBegin returns when a task is in flight: before, a warm-up running when a build started left the build without a background task, and the warm-up's ticker then called
#         BackgroundEnd, which would end whatever task was in flight.
#       - WarmChanged: one main-thread post in flight at a time (Interlocked), redraws coalesced to at most 2 a second (Coalescer, 500 ms); it fired a BeginInvokeOnMainThread per library before.
#       - WarmStrip(game, sheet): a 12 pt accent line (GameWarm.Status) + a UIProgressView (GameWarm.Fraction) when `game` is the one being prepared (title id = GameWarm.ActiveGame); the views are kept
#         in two lists and updated in place by UpdateWarmViews (called from ShowWarm); the shelf is redrawn (RefreshScreen, coalesced) only when the game being prepared changes (a run starts or ends).
#         Accessibility ids: game-warm-status / game-warm-progress, sheet-warm-status / sheet-warm-progress.
#   * GameAppShelf.cs: the Library tile (under name and size) and row (under the chip) add WarmStrip; RefreshShelf clears the shelf's bar list; the game sheet's SHADERS block shows the strip and
#       a line saying the app prepares the shaders the game asks for first by itself after a build ("Prepare the shaders…" stays, for the whole library). Play is untouched: available while it runs; a
#       tap goes through BeginRun -> StopWarm (or, right after a build, through "Close and play", which ends the process: up to ~4 KB of warmed.tsv rows not yet flushed are redone next time, as hits).
#   * GameAppBackground.cs: BackgroundEndWarm(success) ends the task only when it is a warm-up's; BackgroundTick takes GameWarm.Fraction (it parsed "N of M" from the status, which the separators would
#       break); a refused submit says "the shader preparation runs only while iBuilder is open" for a warm-up.
#   * GameAppMain.cs: two hook lines only: the launch call (StartWarmAtLaunch, comment) and the build end (`if (!bootOnly) { RemoveSourceAfterBuild(name, outcome); PrepareAfterBuild(outcome); }`).
#
# NOT DONE / KNOWN EDGES (not verified on a device)
#   * Finish()'s last comment in GameAppMain.cs still says the shaders are never prepared at the end of a build: left as it is (this patch was limited to the two hook lines there).
#   * A build that ends while iBuilder is in the background: the build's continued-processing task is completed by Finish and the warm-up asks for a new one from the background; iOS may refuse
#     (logged), and the warm-up then runs only while the app is open; the next ordinary launch resumes it anyway.
#   * While a warm-up runs, Export's build cleanup offer is not made ("something else is going on": GameAppBuildCleanupUi.cs counts GameWarm.Active as busy; unchanged here).
#   * A run stopped by the player ("Stop preparing the shaders" in the sheet) resumes at the next ordinary launch: only prepared.tsv ends it.
#   * Whether 4,000 is the right cap, and whether warming in the app leaves the host's first ~500 lines as hits, are to be measured on the phone (the log line gives the count; warmed.tsv the ms).
